+    // Inside a delimited JSON-string body, a character that JSON requires to be
+    // escaped must be emitted as its escape sequence, so the grammar matches the
+    // encoded bytes (e.g. `\t`) rather than a raw byte (a literal tab) that is
+    // invalid inside a JSON string. The returned fragment is the inner content
+    // of an EBNF terminal; the caller wraps it in quotes.
